# 958. Check Completeness of a Binary Tree

**Problem Link:** [Check Completeness of a Binary Tree](https://leetcode.com/problems/check-completeness-of-a-binary-tree/)

## Approach: Level Order Traversal (BFS)

- Use a queue to perform level-order traversal.
- Maintain a boolean variable `found` to track whether a NULL node has been encountered.
- Once a NULL node is found, set `found = true`.
- If any non-NULL node appears after that, the tree is not complete.
- If the traversal finishes without violating this condition, the tree is complete.

## Code

```cpp
class Solution {
public:
    bool isCompleteTree(TreeNode* root) {
        if(!root)return true;

        queue<TreeNode*>q;
        q.push(root);

        bool found = false;

        while(!q.empty()){
            TreeNode* node = q.front();
            q.pop();

            if(!node){
                found = true;
                continue;
            }

            if(found)return false;

            q.push(node->left);
            q.push(node->right);
        }

        return true;
    }
};
```

## Complexity Analysis

- **Time Complexity:** O(N) — Each node is processed once.
- **Space Complexity:** O(N) — Queue stores nodes during BFS.

## Key Observation

In a complete binary tree, all levels except possibly the last level must be completely filled, and the last level must be filled from left to right without any gaps.

Once a NULL position is encountered during level-order traversal, no further non-NULL node should exist.
